---
import Annex from '../../layouts/Annex.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';

// Fig 1: three call-pattern timelines. Tick positions computed at build time.
const TICK_X0 = 158, TICK_STEP = 6;
const MD_TICKS = Array.from({ length: 90 }, (_, i) => TICK_X0 + i * TICK_STEP);
const STAGES = [0, 1, 2].map((s) => {
  const x = TICK_X0 + s * 186;
  return {
    load: x,
    ticks: Array.from({ length: 20 }, (_, i) => x + 50 + i * TICK_STEP),
  };
});
const PHONON_ROWS = Array.from({ length: 7 }, (_, i) => 222 + i * 14);
---
<Annex
  title="The Callers"
  piece="05"
  eyebrow="Builder's Annex · 05 · Methods"
  dek="One call has a cost profile. A method makes a million of them — in an order, at a size, with state carried between — and that pattern, more than the model, decides what serving costs."
  prevHref="/annex/the-fine-tuning-problem"
  prevLabel="The Fine-Tuning Problem"
>
  <p>
    <a href={url('/annex/the-performance')}>Where the Time Goes</a> priced one call: neighbor
    list, graph assembly, forward, backward. Nobody buys one call. The unit a user brings is a
    method — molecular dynamics, a free-energy calculation, a phonon spectrum — and a method is a
    policy for calling the potential: how many calls, at what size, in what order, and what
    survives between them. Two methods can run the same model on the same GPU and present bills an
    order of magnitude apart, because one pattern buries the serving stack's fixed costs under big
    payloads and the other lays them bare.
  </p>

  <p>
    Those fixed costs are easiest to see when the model is served out-of-process, which is
    Rootstock's design: LAMMPS keeps the atoms, a worker in its own venv keeps the model, and a
    socket carries positions one way and forces the other. The socket is not the problem. Measured against an
    in-process call on an 864-atom system, the round trip costs about 4%. Then a real method
    arrives.
  </p>

  <p>
    One run, from a day on Princeton's Della cluster — an anecdote, not a benchmark. Iron, bcc, 250
    atoms, MatterSim-v1-5M on a slice of an A100, driven by calphy, a free-energy workflow,
    through LAMMPS and Rootstock's pair style: 91 ms per MD step, with LAMMPS's own timing putting
    99.98% of it in <code>Pair</code>, the round trip to the model. The same checkpoint wrapped
    into LAMMPS's in-process ML-IAP interface moves the same system roughly five to ten times
    faster. The socket's few percent cannot explain a factor of five, even granting that its share
    grows as the system shrinks. Where did the rest go?
  </p>

  <p>
    Into per-call fixed costs. The worker serves the model behind its ASE calculator, the generic
    Python interface every family ships. That route pays full price on every call: it rebuilds the
    model's neighbor graph from scratch, in Python, and copies positions host-to-device and forces
    device-to-host. A native integration — the MACE–LAMMPS Kokkos path is the documented example —
    keeps the arrays resident on the GPU and reuses its neighbor list across many steps, rebuilding
    only when atoms have drifted far enough to invalidate it. (The forward pass itself is eager
    PyTorch either way, hundreds of small kernel launches neither path avoids.) The two paths run
    the same network. They differ in everything around it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Those surrounding costs are fixed per call; the forward pass scales with atom count. At 250
    atoms the fixed cost
    dominates and the served model runs several times slower than the hardware allows. At ten
    thousand atoms the same fixed cost disappears under the forward pass. Nothing about the serving
    changed; the payload did. So "what does serving cost?" has no answer at the model level. It is
    a property of the caller: how many calls, how large each one, and what the method lets you keep
    warm in between.
  </p>

  <h2>The baseline: molecular dynamics</h2>

  <p>
    Plain MD is one whole-system call per step, strictly sequential — each call consumes the forces
    of the last — for 10⁵ to 10⁷ steps. A relaxation is the same shape, shorter: a few hundred calls
    walking one structure downhill. This is the pattern everything else is measured against, and
    its defining feature is what persists between calls. In a native integration, nearly
    everything: the neighbor list carries a skin margin and survives many steps, the arrays never
    leave the GPU, the process never restarts. The atoms barely move between calls, and the stack
    is built to exploit that.
  </p>

  <p>
    A served model keeps the process warm — the worker loads the model once and holds it — but the
    protocol forgets everything else. Each step, the full cell and every position cross the socket;
    Rootstock's protocol even re-sends the species list every cycle, because it resets to its
    initial state after every force return. The calculator behind the socket treats each call as a
    new configuration: new graph, new transfers. MD makes more calls than any method here except
    the Monte Carlo pathology at the end. Whether you notice the multiplied tax is the payload question
    again: at a few thousand atoms the forward pass hides the tax, at a few hundred it is most of
    the bill.
  </p>

  <h2>Enhanced sampling changes the physics, not the call pattern</h2>

  <p>
    Metadynamics and umbrella sampling sound like they should cost more: they steer a simulation
    toward rare events by adding forces the potential never produced. The canonical route is
    PLUMED, and PLUMED is a post-force hook (the engines' native restraint fixes work the same
    way). Each step, the engine computes forces exactly as in plain MD — one
    whole-system call. Then PLUMED reads the positions, computes its collective variables (the
    reaction progress boiled down to a few numbers), and adds a bias force into the force array
    already sitting there. The potential is never consulted twice. However exotic the sampling, the
    served model sees plain MD; the bias only decides where the trajectory goes.
  </p>

  <p>
    The one thing enhanced sampling changes is multiplicity. Multi-walker metadynamics and
    replica-exchange methods run tens of replicas at once, each an independent MD stream making its
    own per-step calls. That is a capacity question, not a latency one, and for a served model it
    is the good kind of demand: independent streams keep every worker busy.
  </p>

  <h2>Free energies: three mechanisms stack</h2>

  <p>
    calphy computes free energies by thermodynamic integration: run MD while gradually switching
    the system's energy function — the Hamiltonian — between the potential you care about and a
    reference whose free energy is known exactly, and integrate the work along the way. From the
    serving side it is the method that finds every fixed cost at once.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>It is a staged workflow.</strong> A calphy calculation is a sequence of separate
    LAMMPS instances — equilibration and switching arrive as distinct runs, the system handed off
    on disk between them. In-process, that costs a process launch. Served, every new instance spawns a new worker, and
    the worker reloads the model: 30 to 60 seconds of stall at the top of each stage. The per-call
    fixed cost has a workflow-scale sibling, the per-process fixed cost, and a method that respawns
    its engine per stage pays it per stage. Long stages amortize the reload; a sweep of short
    calculations — melting points bracketed across ten temperatures — pays it constantly.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>λ-mixing enters two ways, one free and one doubled.</strong> The switching needs a
    mixed Hamiltonian, part model and part reference, and where the mixing happens decides what it
    costs. For a solid reference, <code>fix ti/spring</code> mixes after the call: the pair style
    runs once, then the fix rescales the returned forces by (1−λ) and adds λ-weighted spring forces
    pulling each atom toward its lattice site. Post-force, like PLUMED: one model evaluation per
    step, zero extra serving cost. calphy's alchemical mode instead overlays two pair styles with
    <code>pair_style hybrid/scaled</code>, and both are evaluated every step — switching between
    two potentials means paying for two potentials. (Its temperature-scaling mode also goes through
    <code>hybrid/scaled</code>, but scales a single potential by λ: still one evaluation.) Same λ
    on the page; a factor of two apart on the wire.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Its loops live at small N.</strong> Free-energy runs use a few hundred to a few
    thousand atoms — 250 is an ordinary solid cell, and the physics converges there, so nobody pays
    for more. And calphy adds convergence machinery of its own: equilibration runs until pressure
    averages settle to tolerance, which can add tens of thousands of steps before any switching
    begins. Many sequential calls, smallest possible payload: precisely the regime where the fixed
    per-call cost is the bill.
  </p>

  <p>
    Now the 91 ms step is legible. A 250-atom call pays the full fixed tax — graph rebuild,
    transfers, launch overhead — on a forward pass too small to hide any of it, tens of thousands
    of times per stage, with a model reload between stages. The method did nothing wrong; it makes
    the calls its physics needs. It makes them in the shape serving handles worst.
  </p>

  <h2>The rest of the zoo</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Nudged elastic band.</strong> NEB finds a transition path by stringing k copies of the
    system — images — between two end states and relaxing the string. Every optimization step needs
    forces on all k images, but the images couple only through cheap spring terms computed from
    their neighbors' current positions. Given those positions, the k model calls are independent:
    sequential across iterations, batchable within one. LAMMPS runs each image on its own
    partition; a served model can just as well fan the k calls across workers or batch them into
    one forward pass.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Phonons.</strong> A phonon spectrum by finite displacement is the friendliest caller in
    the zoo. phonopy generates a symmetry-reduced set of displaced supercells — the crystal tiled
    out into a large cell, one atom nudged in each copy — a handful to a few hundred of them, at
    hundreds to thousands of atoms apiece, and each needs exactly one static force calculation. No ordering, no state, payloads big enough for the forward pass to dominate:
    embarrassingly parallel single points. If you designed a workload to flatter a served model,
    it would look like this.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>High-throughput screening.</strong> Relaxing ten thousand candidate structures is ten
    thousand short sequential runs, each a few hundred calls at tens to hundreds of atoms —
    individually, deep in fixed-cost territory. But the structures are independent, so the farm
    batches across them: concatenate many small graphs into one forward pass per step, the batched
    relaxation that torch-sim and FAIR-chem ship. Sequential within a structure, parallel across
    the farm; the batch turns the smallest payloads into one large one.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Monte Carlo.</strong> The worst case. Each trial move displaces
    or swaps one atom and asks for the energy change. A classical short-ranged potential answers
    locally, recomputing a handful of pair terms; the move is nearly free, which is why MC sweeps
    of thousands of trials per atom are routine. An MLIP's answer is also local in principle — the
    change propagates only to the receptive field, a few cutoffs out — but a model served
    whole-system-per-call cannot say so. The interface answers one question, the energy of
    everything, so one single-atom trial costs one full call and a sweep of N trials costs N of
    them. In-process MC hits the same wall — LAMMPS's own Monte Carlo fixes fall back to
    whole-system energies for many-body potentials — but serving removes the last escape hatch: an
    interface that recomputed only the affected patch would collapse the cost, and none offers one.
  </p>

  <h2>Six axes predict the bill</h2>

  <p>
    Every pattern above is a position along six axes, and the axes are checkable from a method's
    documentation before anything runs:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Calls per unit of science.</strong> A phonon spectrum is a few hundred calls; a free
      energy is hundreds of thousands; a melting-point sweep, millions. This number multiplies
      everything else.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Atoms per call.</strong> The payload that either hides the fixed per-call cost or
      exposes it. Below a few hundred atoms, overhead is the workload.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sequential or batchable.</strong> Does call n+1 need call n's output? MD does; phonon
      displacements, NEB images within an iteration, and screening farms do not, and independent
      calls are the only ones a server can aggregate.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What survives between calls.</strong> One warm process for a million steps, or a
      model reload per stage? Staged workflows pay startup repeatedly; long trajectories pay it
      once.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What each call must return.</strong> Energy only, forces, or forces plus the stress
      on the cell. A Monte Carlo trial needs the energy alone — no backward pass, roughly half the
      work — but a whole-system serving contract returns energy and forces regardless, so the
      discount goes unclaimed. A barostat needs the stress, which not every serving interface
      returns.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Where auxiliary forces enter.</strong> A post-force hook — PLUMED's bias,
      <code>ti/spring</code>'s springs — costs zero model calls. An overlaid second potential is
      evaluated every step. The distinction hides in one line of a LAMMPS input.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    (If you serve language models: sequential MD is autoregressive decoding, a phonon batch is
    scoring a dataset of independent prompts, and each method sits somewhere between the two.)
  </p>

