          <>
            <rect x={st.load} y="148" width="42" height="18" class="f-wash s-acc" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3" />
            {st.ticks.map((x) => <line x1={x} y1={148} x2={x} y2={166} class="s-acc" stroke-width="1" />)}
          </>
        ))}
        <text x="120" y="188" class="t-xs t-lab">fresh LAMMPS instance per stage · model reloads each time · 250-atom calls</text>

        <!-- lane: phonons -->
        <text x="108" y="252" class="t-s t-ink" text-anchor="end">phonons</text>
        <rect x="120" y="222" width="30" height="94" class="f-wash s-acc" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3" />
        {PHONON_ROWS.map((y) => <rect x={158} y={y} width="9" height="10" class="f-acc" />)}
        <text x="120" y="338" class="t-xs t-lab">k independent single points · no ordering · batch or fan out</text>

        <!-- legend -->
        <rect x="450" y="222" width="14" height="12" class="f-wash s-acc" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3" />
        <text x="472" y="232" class="t-xs t-lab">model load / reload</text>
        <line x1="456" y1="248" x2="456" y2="260" class="s-acc" stroke-width="1.4" />
        <text x="472" y="258" class="t-xs t-lab">one force call</text>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <figcaption class="afig-cap"><b>Fig 1</b> Three callers against the same served model. Plain MD loads once and streams sequential calls forever; the per-call tax is its only serving cost, and payload size decides whether you notice. calphy re-pays the model load at every stage and makes its calls at small N, so both fixed costs surface at once. Phonon displacements have no ordering at all — the calls land as one batch, and a served model handles them as well as anything can.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Before serving a method you have never heard of, read its call pattern along these six axes.
    The answers say where the time will go before the first step runs. Where the Time Goes said
    the model choice is the systems choice. So is the method choice.
  </p>
</Annex>
